    subgraph CL["CLUSTER — pack close together"]
      direction LR
      subgraph AZ1["One AZ"]
        C1["EC2"] --- C2["EC2"] --- C3["EC2"]
        C4["EC2"] --- C5["EC2"] --- C6["EC2"]
      end
    end
    subgraph SP["SPREAD — separate individual instances"]
      direction LR
      R1["Rack 1<br/>EC2-A"]
      R2["Rack 2<br/>EC2-B"]
      R3["Rack 3<br/>EC2-C"]
    end
    subgraph PA["PARTITION — separate sets of racks"]
      direction LR
      P1["Partition 1<br/>Rack set A<br/>Many EC2s"]
      P2["Partition 2<br/>Rack set B<br/>Many EC2s"]
      P3["Partition 3<br/>Rack set C<br/>Many EC2s"]
    end
    subgraph PT["PRECISION TIME"]
      T1["EC2 instances on supported hardware"] --> T2["Enhanced time source / PTP clock"]
    end
```

*The diagram is a conceptual model, not a literal AWS rack layout.*

### 5.1 Cluster placement group

**Meaning:** EC2 packs instances close together within **one Availability Zone** in a high-bisection-bandwidth network segment.

**Why choose it?** Low network latency and high throughput between instances. It is useful when most traffic is east-west (instance-to-instance), for example tightly coupled HPC, distributed compute or large data-processing jobs.

**Important details:**

- A Cluster placement group cannot span multiple AZs.
- Instances are **not guaranteed to be on one rack**. “Close together” means close network placement, not a promise of one physical rack.
- Use supported instance types and Enhanced Networking (ENA); for supported HPC workloads, Elastic Fabric Adapter (EFA) may also be appropriate.
- Available network performance depends on the instance types and networking features. Do not memorise the slide's old “10 Gbps” statement as a universal guarantee.
- Capacity can be difficult to obtain, especially when adding instances later or mixing instance types. AWS recommends launching the required fleet together and using the same instance type where practical.

**Trade-off:** Great performance, but one AZ is the placement boundary. An AZ outage can affect the whole group. Do not choose Cluster when the primary requirement is AZ-level isolation.

**Example:** 16 tightly coupled compute nodes exchange large amounts of data and need low-latency node-to-node communication.

### 5.2 Spread placement group

**Meaning:** EC2 places each instance on distinct underlying hardware—at rack level in AWS Regions—so that one rack failure is less likely to affect multiple instances in the group.

**Think:** one critical server per separate rack.

**Key limits and facts:**

- Rack-level Spread placement groups are supported in AWS Regions and can span multiple AZs in the **same Region**.
- Maximum **seven running instances per AZ per rack-level Spread placement group**.
- In a three-AZ Region, a single group could therefore support up to 21 running instances in total, provided seven are placed in each AZ and capacity is available.
- Host-level Spread is supported on AWS Outposts; it is not the standard Regional EC2 Spread option.
- If you need more than seven instances in one AZ, AWS recommends considering multiple Spread groups. Separate groups do not guarantee isolation from each other, but each group provides its own spread guarantee.

**Use it for:** a small number of critical instances that must be isolated from one another, such as a small set of highly available controllers or important infrastructure nodes.

**Trade-off:** Strong individual isolation, but the seven-per-AZ limit makes it unsuitable for very large fleets.

### 5.3 Partition placement group

**Meaning:** EC2 divides a large workload into logical **partitions**. Each partition gets its own set of racks. Instances in different partitions do not share those racks.

This is the part that often causes confusion:

- A **partition is a group of racks**, not one EC2 instance and not necessarily one rack.
- Multiple EC2 instances can be in the same partition. They can share that partition's rack set.
- Another partition uses a different rack set, so a rack-level failure in Partition 1 should not affect the hardware used by Partition 2.

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    subgraph AZ["Availability Zone (one example AZ)"]
      subgraph P1["Partition 1 — rack set A"]
        A1["Rack A1"]
        A2["Rack A2"]
        A3["EC2 instances"]
      end
      subgraph P2["Partition 2 — rack set B"]
        B1["Rack B1"]
        B2["Rack B2"]
        B3["EC2 instances"]
      end
      subgraph P3["Partition 3 — rack set C"]
        C1["Rack C1"]
        C2["Rack C2"]
        C3["EC2 instances"]
      end
    end
```

**Key facts:**

- A Partition placement group can span multiple AZs in the same Region.
- Maximum **seven partitions per AZ** in one group.
- The number of EC2 instances is constrained by account/service quotas and available capacity—not a fixed “hundreds only” ceiling.
- AWS attempts to distribute instances evenly across the partitions you specify, but does not guarantee a perfectly even distribution.
- EC2 exposes the partition number through instance metadata. Topology-aware applications can use it to place replicas in different partitions.

**Use it for:** large distributed and replicated systems such as Kafka, Cassandra, HDFS and HBase.

**Why is it useful for Kafka/Cassandra?** These systems replicate data across nodes. If replicas are deliberately placed in different partitions, a rack or partition-level infrastructure issue is less likely to remove all copies at once. The application must still configure replication correctly; the placement group does not replicate data for you.

### 5.4 Precision time placement group

**Meaning:** places instances on supported hardware with direct access to high-precision time sources in AWS infrastructure.

It provides:

- An enhanced local Amazon Time Sync Service source.
- A PTP Hardware Clock (PHC) device for microsecond-accurate synchronization on supported Linux instances.
- Hardware packet timestamping for nanosecond-resolution network measurements on supported Linux instances.

**Use it for:** financial timestamping, distributed databases that need accurate transaction ordering, and distributed systems that compare event times.

**Do not confuse it with:** a low-latency Cluster group or a failure-isolation Spread group. Its special property is **time accuracy**. Hardware availability and supported instance families must be checked; a launch can fail if the AZ does not have suitable hardware. There is no additional charge for the Precision Time placement group itself.

**Advanced note:** AWS also supports creating a Cluster placement group with a parent Precision Time placement group when you need both cluster-style network placement and precision-time hardware.

### Placement group decision table

| Requirement / scenario | Choose | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Lowest network latency between tightly coupled EC2 nodes | **Cluster** | Packs instances close in one AZ |
| Small number of critical instances must be on different racks | **Spread** | Distinct underlying racks; max 7 running instances per AZ per Regional group |
| Large Kafka/Cassandra/HDFS fleet needs rack-set isolation | **Partition** | Many instances across logical partitions; up to 7 partitions per AZ |
| Microsecond-accurate clock synchronization or precise timestamps | **Precision time** | Access to enhanced time source / PTP hardware |

### Placement group rules to remember

- An instance can belong to **only one placement group at a time**.
- A placement group influences relative placement; it does not replace load balancing, replication, Auto Scaling or multi-AZ design.
- Placement groups themselves have no creation charge; the EC2 instances and associated resources are billed normally.
- Launching or restarting can fail if suitable capacity is unavailable. A placement group is not a capacity reservation.
- If no placement group is specified, EC2 manages placement and attempts to reduce correlated failures by spreading instances across the underlying hardware.

**30-second interview answer:** “Placement groups control how EC2 instances are positioned relative to one another. Cluster packs instances in one AZ for low latency and high throughput. Spread isolates a small number of instances on separate racks, up to seven per AZ in a Regional group. Partition separates large fleets into rack-isolated groups and can span AZs in one Region. Precision time provides access to high-accuracy time sources. I choose based on whether the requirement is performance, failure isolation or clock accuracy.”

