## 9. Quick revision sheet

| Term | Remember this |
|---|---|
| Region | Geographic AWS area; contains multiple AZs |
| AZ | Isolated infrastructure location; one or more data centers |
| AZ ID | Stable physical AZ identifier across AWS accounts |
| Private IPv4 | Internal VPC address; retained through Stop/Start |
| Auto-assigned public IPv4 | Released on Stop/Hibernate; new one on Start |
| Elastic IP | Static, Region-scoped public IPv4; billable |
| Cluster | Pack close; performance; one AZ; not same-rack guarantee |
| Spread | Separate instances on distinct racks; max 7 running/AZ/group in Regions |
| Partition | Separate rack sets per partition; max 7 partitions/AZ; supports large fleets |
| Precision time | Enhanced time source, PTP clock and packet timestamping on supported Linux instances |
| ENI | Virtual NIC with IPs, SGs and MAC; subnet/AZ-bound |
| Secondary ENI | Can move between EC2 instances in the same AZ |
| Source/destination check | Disable on an instance that forwards traffic (NAT/router/firewall) |
| Stop | RAM lost; EBS data persists; auto public IPv4 released |
| Hibernate | RAM saved to encrypted EBS root volume; resume processes on start |
| Terminate | Instance deleted; EBS volumes with DeleteOnTermination enabled are deleted |

### Common mistakes to avoid

- Do not say that a Cluster group guarantees instances share one physical rack.
- Do not say that a Spread group supports unlimited instances; Regional rack-level Spread is capped at seven running instances per AZ per group.
- Do not confuse a Partition with one EC2 instance or one rack. A partition is a logical group with its own rack set.
- Do not say an ENI can move between AZs. It cannot.
- Do not say a public IPv4 address is free or permanent. Auto-assigned public IPv4 changes on Stop/Start, and public IPv4 addresses are charged.
- Do not confuse an EIP (public IPv4 endpoint) with an ENI (virtual network card).
- Do not say Stop preserves RAM. Only Hibernate saves RAM state.
- Do not use placement groups as a substitute for data replication, load balancing or multi-AZ design.

