## 10. AWS CLI and metadata commands worth knowing

### Show AZ names and their stable AZ IDs

```bash
aws ec2 describe-availability-zones \
  --query 'AvailabilityZones[].{Name:ZoneName,AZ_ID:ZoneId,State:State}' \
  --output table
```

### List network interfaces

```bash
aws ec2 describe-network-interfaces \
  --query 'NetworkInterfaces[].{ENI:NetworkInterfaceId,AZ:AvailabilityZone,PrivateIP:PrivateIpAddress,Status:Status,Instance:Attachment.InstanceId}' \
  --output table
```

### Read the instance's AZ ID with IMDSv2

Run from inside the EC2 instance:

```bash
TOKEN=$(curl -X PUT \
  "http://169.254.169.254/latest/api/token" \
  -H "X-aws-ec2-metadata-token-ttl-seconds: 21600")

curl -H "X-aws-ec2-metadata-token: $TOKEN" \
  http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/placement/availability-zone-id
```

### Read the placement-group name and partition number

```bash
# Placement group name (empty if the instance is not in a placement group)
curl -H "X-aws-ec2-metadata-token: $TOKEN" \
  http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/placement/group-name

# Partition number (relevant to Partition placement groups)
curl -H "X-aws-ec2-metadata-token: $TOKEN" \
  http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/placement/partition-number
```

Use IMDSv2 and protect access to instance metadata in production. These examples assume the token command above has been run in the same shell.

